plan Types
title   Type inference and requirements
status  archived
goal
    Define Flow State's migrated inference, variance, Effect-channel
    propagation, Implementation closure, actor/ref/lease, host, Story, and
    compile-proof requirements. The rewrite is incomplete; these are target
    requirements, not implementation claims. This Plan owns migrated semantics under
    @plan.Verification.D1; source contracts provide provenance and trace IDs. Public
    authoring shapes belong
    to @plan.PublicSurface.O2 and @plan.PublicSurface.O3. Identity and lifecycle
    terms belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O1, @plan.RuntimeV2.O3, and @plan.RuntimeV2.O4.

outcomes
    O1  Definition, machine, event, input, and memory inference
        - definition preserves literal IDs and recursive exact state tokens.
          It preserves event names, parameter tuples, result payloads, context values, and named
          operation families. It infers input and memory.
          It requires no as const, satisfies, explicit generics, or wrappers.
          It returns the direct Effect Result.Result<DefinitionFromConfig<Config>,
          ReturnType<typeof Diagnostic.Failure>> value.
          Each event token call returns Result.Result<EventEnvelope<...>, ReturnType<typeof
          Diagnostic.Failure>>; EventOf<Definition> is the successful event-envelope union, not a
          union of Result wrappers. StateOf/EventOf accept Definition or Machine and return the same
          exact unions. Event tokens are nominal; structurally similar functions/objects are not
          assignable. Definition literal types preserve accepted exact spelling. Ten-level
          validation is synchronous at construction; type inference does not encode recursive depth
          arithmetic.

        - machine(definitionResult, callback) matches the Definition Result before callback
          execution. It anchors inference to its successful value and supplies exact S, E, O,
          onContext, onMemory, invalidate, and clear. Use NoInfer or equivalent one-way inference.
          Local states and on keys check against the recursive definition.
          Defaults, targets, outcomes, and capabilities use its exact tokens.
          Compound handlers form one event protocol.
          Ancestor plus descendant handlers for the same active-path event are ambiguous.
          No leaf fallback or override inference applies. State is the exact active leaf.
          matches accepts a leaf or ancestor. The callback cannot enlarge or replace the definition
          universe.

        - Input and Memory come only from memory: ({ input }: { input: Input }) => Memory. Omission
          means Input=void and empty memory. Input is supplied once to a fresh actor and only the
          initializer consumes it; behavior receives memory/context/state/events, not original
          input. Restore installs memory without initializer or input replay. App.M admission is
          independent of input; app compilation makes no actor. InputOf/MemoryOf are exact for
          Definition or Machine. Reject type-only memory markers, separate initialMemory,
          behavior-returned memory, implicit input classification, authored input for void machines,
          and input mutation/classification.

        - Event transition callbacks receive the exact causal event, state, memory, snapshot, and
          primitive registries.
        - updateMemory returns only Partial<Memory>.
        - Guard, updateMemory, and actions see the same pre-turn snapshot and event.
        - They never see candidate memory.
        - reenter accepts only an exact active-state token.
        - Reject unknown fields/values and Boolean, foreign, inactive, or outside-boundary reentry.
        - Reject candidate-memory visibility.

        - Public and type-focused examples appear above and under @plan.PublicSurface.O2.
          CONTRACT.md §2 examples remain proposed and unaccepted; they add no authority. Some
          proposal examples repeat Plan target code, but keep proposal status explicit. Definition
          and runtime vocabulary belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O1.

        Example:
        ```ts
        import { Result } from "effect";
        import { definition, Diagnostic } from "flow-state";
        
        type EventResultValue<Value> = Value extends Result.Result<infer Success, ReturnType<typeof Diagnostic.Failure>> ? Success : never;
        
        const TodoResult = definition({
          id: "Todos/Editor",
          states: ["READY", { SAVING: ["REQUESTED", "COMMITTING"] }],
          events: {
            SaveRequested: (title: string) => ({ title }),
            SaveCompleted: "bare",
          },
          operations: {
            todo: todoResource,
          },
        });
        if (Result.isFailure(TodoResult)) {
          // handle TodoResult.failure
        } else {
          const Todo = TodoResult.success;
          type _State = Expect<
            Equal<
              StateOf<typeof Todo>,
              typeof Todo.S.READY | typeof Todo.S.SAVING.S.REQUESTED | typeof Todo.S.SAVING.S.COMMITTING
            >
          >;
          type _Event = Expect<
            Equal<
              EventOf<typeof Todo>,
              | EventResultValue<ReturnType<typeof Todo.E.SaveRequested>>
              | EventResultValue<ReturnType<typeof Todo.E.SaveCompleted>>
            >
          >;
        }
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        machine(TodoResult, ({ S, E, O, onContext, onMemory }) => ({
          default: S.READY,
          states: { READY: { on: { SaveRequested: { target: S.SAVING.S.REQUESTED } } } },
        }));
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const EditorResult = definition({
          id: "Todos/Editor",
          states: ["READY", "SAVING"],
          events: { SaveRequested: "bare" },
          memory: ({ input }: { input: { todoId: string } }) => ({
            todoId: input.todoId,
            draft: "",
          }),
        });
        if (Result.isSuccess(EditorResult)) {
          const Editor = EditorResult.success;
          type _Input = Expect<Equal<InputOf<typeof Editor>, { todoId: string }>>;
          type _Memory = Expect<Equal<MemoryOf<typeof Editor>, { todoId: string; draft: string }>>;
        }
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        SaveRequested: {
          target: S.SAVING,
          updateMemory: ({ event }) => ({ draft: event.title }),
          actions: ({ event, memory }) => [O.save.commit({ title: event.title, id: memory.id })],
          reenter: S.SAVING,
        }
        ```

    O2  Resource, transaction, stream, and failure inference
        - Each Resource carries exact executable P, canonical K, success A, typed failure E, and
          requirements R. Lookup/config/plans preserve P; key/getData/getState use K. No resource
          ref, second key, custom hash, or custom equality exists. persist defaults false; true opts
          committed canonical entries into Persistence. K follows the exact copied
          canonical-container grammar, discriminator, bounds, and diagnostics of
          @plan.PublicSurface.O3, with no second type-level interpretation. Exact P/K/A/E/R
          propagate through descriptor, named O, machine, app, runtime, fixture, and Story.

        - Transaction carries exact P/A/E/R and key-derived P/K. Binding it in actor O preserves
          those types and captures no parent machine, memory, event, selector, route, preview, or
          invalidation type. Its methods are exact key/passive getState/commit/cancel. K defaults to
          []; commit is action-only. Concurrency is reject | cancel | allow | serialize. Failure
          mapping is absent when E=never; defect/interruption remain distinct. Only explicit setData
          writes update canonical data. Persistence is false by default; true opts actor-owned
          durable facts only when the owning stable actor is persistable. Reject missing/widened
          params, failure mapping with E=never, implicit result promotion, and local-actor
          durability inferred from the descriptor alone.

        - Stream carries exact P/K/V/E/R with no parent selector, routed event, or child actor.
        - Its family is passive key/getState plus continuing subscribe, with no actor cancel.
        - State projection retains status, hasValue, latest V when present, emissionCount,
          generation, and terminal status.
        - The exact closed union and field optionality belong to @plan.PublicSurface.O3.
        - Emissions become durable only through mapped events or explicit writes.
        - persist defaults false.
        - When true, persist may retain only latest, terminal, generation, and declaration facts
          for a persistable stable owner.
        - Fibers, scopes, cursors, transports, and history are non-persistable.
        - Hydration uses live P after pending outcomes drain and does not replay.
        - Terminal streams do not restart.
        - Missing P fails closed with a precise diagnostic.
        - Reject parent-type graphs, stream cancellation, terminal restart, K reconstruction, and
          widened completion values.

        - Operation outcomes classify the complete Effect Cause into typed failure E, defect, or
          interruption. Defect has precedence if any defect exists.
          Otherwise typed failure wins if any failure exists; interruption wins only by itself.
          Production uses Effect.exit rather than
          Effect.result. Never flatten defect/interruption into E. Canonical Diagnostic
          classification and Cause-to-diagnostic projection are owned by @plan.DiagnosticsV2.O2.

        - Public generic and state shapes belong to @plan.PublicSurface.O3. Examples appear above
          and under that owner. CONTRACT.md §3 examples remain proposed and unaccepted. Some repeat
          Plan target code; the distinct transaction and passive-read forms appear under
          @plan.PublicSurface.O3.

        Example:
        ```ts
        const project = resource({
          id: "projects.by-id",
          key: ({ id }: { id: string }) => [id],
          lookup: ({ id }, { signal }): Effect.Effect<Project, "missing", ProjectRepo> =>
            Effect.gen(function* () {
              const repo = yield* ProjectRepo;
              return yield* repo.get(id, { signal });
            }),
        });
        project.getData(project.key({ id: "project-1" }));
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        type SaveParams = { id: string; title: string };
        
        const save = transaction({
          id: "projects.save",
          key: ({ id }: SaveParams) => [id],
          commit: ({ id, title }: SaveParams, { signal }): Effect.Effect<Project, SaveError, ProjectRepo> =>
            ProjectRepo.save(id, title, { signal }),
        });
        
        O.save.commit(
          { id: "project-1", title: "Updated" },
          {
            outcomes: {
              success: (value) => ProjectEvents.E.Saved(value),
              failure: (error) => ProjectEvents.E.SaveFailed(error),
            },
          },
        );
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        type ProgressInput = { submissionId: string };
        
        const progress = stream({
          id: "projects.progress",
          key: ({ submissionId }: ProgressInput) => [submissionId],
          subscribe: ({ submissionId }: ProgressInput, { signal }) =>
            Effect.gen(function* () {
              const progressClient = yield* ProgressClient;
              return yield* progressClient.progress(submissionId, { signal });
            }),
        });
        ```

    O3  Requirement closure and typed Implementation providers
        - Resource, transaction, stream, machine, module, app, fixture, Story, and model carry
          hidden covariant requirements. RequirementsOf<T> exposes the application requirement union
          without provider implementation branding. Descriptor carriers retain raw R; Flow owns
          Scope.Scope via Effect.scoped, so RequirementsOf removes Scope. Machine unions every
          reachable named descriptor/machine graph; module unions its exact record; app unions exact
          App.M. Context selectors add typed provider edges, not parentage. Passive reads/callbacks
          add no requirements. App compilation makes no actor and rejects dynamicMachines/automatic
          roots.

        - Carriers are precomputed, private and covariant. RequirementsOf reads a carrier directly
          rather than rewalking authored config/selectors/outcomes/plans. Direction is descriptors
          -> machine bindings -> named module -> app. Public Machine does not retain complete
          authored config as a generic; there is no child-machine carrier edge. The graph is
          acyclic/bounded and runtime never expands AppPlan.

        - Public Implementation is a typed provider graph under namespace Implementation.
        - succeed(service,value) is a complete synchronous, non-failing provider.
        - effect(service,acquire) acquires at most once per Runtime.
        - Its typed error is ImplementationError; Runtime owns its scope and finalizer.
        - merge composes providers without changing service identity.
        - Provider construction is inert: it acquires no services and starts no work.
        - Duplicate service identity rejects; the merge never chooses by order.
        - RuntimeSetup must close RequirementsOf<App>.
        - A partial or remaining graph is a type or readiness failure.
        - Fixtures follow the same rules and override by service identity.
        - Accept complete providers and strict supersets with no remaining input requirements.
        - Reject unresolved inputs, Layer-like opaque semantics, kernel replacement, matchers, and
          control registries.

        - Implementation construction has no runtime effect until Runtime acquisition owns it.
          Fixture Implementations cannot take kernel ownership; their functions preserve authored
          input/success/failure/cancellation/lifetime, while kernels own
          admission/completion/writes/projections/evidence.

        - The public admission/provider surface belongs to @plan.PublicSurface.O4 and
          @plan.PublicSurface.O5. Examples above use private carrier notation; none implies that
          helper aliases are exported. AppPlan and module identity belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O2.

        Example:
        ```ts
        type _MachineR = Expect<Equal<RequirementsOf<typeof projectMachine>, ProjectRepo | AuditLog>>;
        type _AppR = Expect<Equal<RequirementsOf<typeof ProjectApp>, ProjectRepo | AuditLog>>;
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        type NormalizedCarrier<R> = { _requirements?: (r: R) => R };
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const TodoLive = Implementation.succeed(
          TodoGateway,
          TodoGateway.of({
            list: ({ listId }, { signal }) => fetchTodos(listId, { signal }),
            add: ({ listId, title }, { signal }) => createTodo(listId, title, { signal }),
          }),
        );
        const TodoStory = Implementation.succeed(
          TodoGateway,
          TodoGateway.of({
            list: () => Effect.succeed(initialTodos),
            add: ({ title }) => Effect.succeed({ id: "todo-1", title, completed: false }),
          }),
        );
        const TodoWithClock = Implementation.merge(TodoLive, ClockLive);
        ```

    O4  RuntimeSetup and Effect bridge types
        - Current RuntimeSetup/Runtime is an inert carrier with only app, construct(), and ready().
          construct() is synchronous and inert. ready() has type Effect<void, ImplementationError>.

        - ready() lazily acquires complete Implementation providers sequentially, once per shell.
          It then caches that shell's first terminal Exit exactly once.
          The Exit is success, typed ImplementationError failure, defect, or interruption.
          Later runs reuse the same Exit without retry. Cause preserves these classes.
          Exact typed ImplementationError is not mapped to Diagnostic.

        - The current shell has no persistence, actors, Effect bridge, lifecycle, or disposal.
          Bootstrap, restoration, persistence, actors, bridges, lifecycle, and disposal remain
          future targets.

        - runtimeSetup has two overloads:
          - For requirements never, { app: A } returns RuntimeSetup<A, never>.
          - Otherwise, { app: A, implementation: Implementation<RequirementsOf<A>, IE> }
            returns RuntimeSetup<A, IE>.
          The first overload is valid only when requirements are never. The second requires
          complete closure. Strict provider supersets are valid only with no remaining inputs.

        - Persistence options are commented restoration notation and do not enter the current
          shell. Future create/ensure accept exact App.M machines.
          Lookup uses refs only; it does not create or adopt. persistence(options) returns inert
          Persistence without changing Implementation closure.

        - Future Runtime<App, ImplementationError> bridges runPromise/runPromiseExit only for
          Effects satisfied by installed application Context. Reusable services, workflows, and
          foreign adapters return Effects with A/E/R intact.

        - runPromise*, runSync*, and related runners are permitted only at these boundaries:
          - Runtime bridge
          - Final process, framework, or CLI adapters
          - Tests
          - Explicitly owned shutdown

        - runPromiseExit resolves Promise<Exit.Exit<A, E | ImplementationError>>.
          Acquisition failure remains inside Exit rather than becoming Promise rejection.
          The bridge cannot claim arbitrary services are installed.
          Do not use Promise rejection for Implementation acquisition errors.
          Do not call Effect runners in reusable code.

        - Runtime setup and bridge examples appear above. The temporary shell remains the
          current public boundary; future bridge targets do not claim implementation.
          Runtime ownership belongs to
          @plan.RuntimeV2.O2 and @plan.RuntimeV2.O3.

        Example:
        ```ts
        function runtimeSetup<A extends App>(options: {
          app: A;
          // Deferred until the Persistence owner lands; retained for restoration.
          // persistence?: Persistence;
        }): RuntimeSetup<A, never>;
        function runtimeSetup<A extends App, ImplementationError>(options: {
          app: A;
          implementation: Implementation<RequirementsOf<A>, ImplementationError>;
          // Deferred until the Persistence owner lands; retained for restoration.
          // persistence?: Persistence;
        }): RuntimeSetup<A, ImplementationError>;
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        runtimeSetup({ app: PublicApp });
        runtimeSetup({ app: ProjectApp, implementation: ProjectLive });
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        // @ts-expect-error ProjectRepo remains unsatisfied
        runtimeSetup({ app: ProjectApp });
        // @ts-expect-error remaining DatabaseConfig requirement
        runtimeSetup({ app: ProjectApp, implementation: ProjectImplementationRequiringConfig });
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        type Result<A, E, IE> = Promise<Exit.Exit<A, E | IE>>;
        ```

    O5  Actor family and passive React selectors
        - ActorRef identity is current. Actor handles, snapshots, leases, and lifecycle are future
          targets. The temporary Runtime shell has no actor methods.
          ActorSnapshot remains deferred from active public types.

        - Future Actor preserves StateOf, EventOf, MemoryOf, context, and exact named operation
          families. ref is ActorRef<Machine>; send accepts EventOf.
          snapshot/snapshots expose exact ActorSnapshot. Registry-free reads preserve descriptor/K
          types, and status unions narrow without casts.

        - actorRef(machine,id,{persist?:boolean}) preserves exact machine and stable-ID types.
          persist defaults false; it is not identity and only marks stable eligibility.
          Local opaque refs cannot satisfy a persistable ref.

        - Lifecycle is exactly prepared | active | suspended | disposed.
          Prepared buffers commands. Suspended rejects commands and has no live attachments.
          Disposed is terminal. Actors and refs have no dispose method.

        - Snapshots expose only active FlowIssue summaries. They omit receipts, pending facts,
          binding cursors, raw Cause, and full diagnostics.

        - Future React exports are useActor(machine,options?), useActorByRef(ref), and
          useView(actor,selector).
        - useActor creates a fresh local actor with no stable ID.
        - useActorByRef is lookup-only and grants no lease.

        - useView requires an exact actor handle. It selects atomic passive state, memory, context,
          lifecycle, issues, and bound can/O. Its result preserves exact Value with no implicit
          null or undefined.
          O allows only key/getData/getState and has no comparator.

        - Scalar/non-record equality is complete-value Object.is. Named records use fixed-key,
          field-by-field Object.is. Selectors cannot acquire or mutate.
          They rerun only for matching tracked actor/StoreFanout publication.

        - Actor-family and selector examples appear above. Public ref/lease ownership belongs to
          @plan.RuntimeV2.O3; snapshots and selectors belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O5.

        Example:
        ```ts
        // Future target only; unavailable on the temporary shell:
        // const actor: Actor<Machine> = runtime.getActor(ref);
        // actor.ref satisfies ActorRef<Machine>;
        // actor.send(/* EventOf<Machine> */);
        // actor.getSnapshot();
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const selected = useView(projectActor, ({ state, O }) => ({
          state,
          project: O.project.getData(O.project.key({ id: "project-1" })),
        }));
        // @ts-expect-error machine is not actor handle
        useView(projectMachine, ({ state }) => state);
        ```

    O6  Story, fixture, recipe, and model types
        - Story uses three closed option shapes. InputOptions<M> requires input:InputOf<M> for
          non-void input and input?:never for void input. SelectedContextOptions<M> requires exact
          SelectedContextOf<M> when any key exists, otherwise context?:never.
          ContextBindingOptions<M> requires exact StoryContextBindingsOf<M> when selected keys
          exist, otherwise contextBindings?:never. Each context key maps to an exact compatible
          ActorRef<ProviderMachineAt<M,K>> or StoryActorRecipe. FixtureOptions makes fixtures
          optional when RequirementsOf<Owner> is never and otherwise requires
          FixtureTupleClosing<RequirementsOf<Owner>>. AppStoryOptions is FixtureOptions<App> plus
          maxTurns?, title?, description?, tags:string[]. MachineStoryOptions combines exact input,
          selected context, fixtures, and that metadata. ActorRecipeOptions is exact input plus
          context bindings. These helper aliases, ProviderMachineAt, StoryActorRecipe,
          FixtureTupleClosing, CheckpointEvidence, EndEvidence, and implementation-layer notation
          are specification-only and need not be exported.

        - FixtureDefinition is returned by fixture({id,implementation,seeds?}). Implementation
          provides complete service identities; seeds are preloaded Runtime-owned resource state and
          do not satisfy missing service needs. Fixture providers override App providers by service
          identity; duplicate providers reject instead of choosing by order. A provider constructs
          once per Runtime; Story runs receive fresh scoped provider state. An
          Implementation<Output,ImplementationError> is a complete service provider graph whose
          combined outputs satisfy Output and whose acquisition may fail with ImplementationError.
          It is not a second testing runtime or required helper constructor. It never replaces
          resource/transaction/stream kernels or installs matchers/control registries.

        - StoryPlan<Names> accumulates literal/known string-union checkpoint names.
          NewCheckpointName rejects widened string and duplicate names.
          checkpoint returns a new StoryPlan<Names | Name>.
          Every other builder preserves Names and returns a new plan.
          run(options?:{signal?:AbortSignal}) returns Promise<StoryRun<Names>>.
          StoryRun.checkpoints maps exactly by Names, with existing evidence types and end evidence.
          A widened string cannot erase known names.
          Unknown literal property access rejects; private
          untrusted string lookup checks own-key membership and uses existing FlowUsageError
          semantics, without adding a public dynamic getter/error.

        - StoryActorRecipe is inert exact machine/input/bindings only, with no runtime, mailbox,
          snapshot, operation, disposal, or live handle. Binding keys are declared slots and values
          exact compatible app-owned ActorRef or Story recipe. The same recipe object resolves one
          actor per run; distinct recipe objects are independent. App targets accept an exact recipe
          or stable ref. Materialize via production createActor, retain leases, observe only
          lease.actor, dispose in reverse dependency order. App-owned shared actors remain
          Runtime-owned. Reject missing/extra/foreign/cyclic bindings, machine families as targets,
          foreign refs, and embedded runtime ownership.

        - Fixture Implementation closes all services for App/focused Machine. A Fixture provider
          overrides App provider by identity; duplicates reject. Seeds do not remove requirements
          even if lookup is skipped. Providers replace complete service functions while preserving
          authored input, success, failure, cancellation, and lifetime; never kernels, matchers, or
          registries. Seeds are Runtime-owned preload state. Providers construct once per Runtime;
          Story runs get fresh scoped state unless application ownership shares it. Kernels own
          admission/completion/writes/projections/evidence.

        - model(baseStory,{stateKey}) preserves machine, exact fresh input, selected context,
          fixtures, progress policy, and an empty command tuple.
        - stateKey is pure and receives the exact predicted snapshot.
        - stateKey returns CanonicalKeyInput.
          Candidate events belong to each traversal call.
          path.story extends the base with exact candidate events and empty checkpoints.
          Only getShortestPaths/getSimplePaths are exposed.
          Model rejects app Story, boot, extra commands, non-fresh restoration, impure stateKey,
          and cross-call candidates.
          It runs no Effects and synthesizes no async routes.
          It exposes no replay/provide/clock helpers or named path/result classes.
          FlowStoryExecutionError is the sole named testing runtime class.
          Its package-owned envelope retains complete public Cause under @plan.PublicSurface.O5
          and @plan.Verification.V4. Snapshots and inferred values still hide raw Cause.

        - Story, fixture, recipe, and model examples appear above and under @plan.PublicSurface.O5.
          Runtime identity and recipe ownership belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O3 and @plan.RuntimeV2.O5.

        Example:
        ```ts
        type InputOptions<M> =
          InputOf<M> extends void ? { input?: never } : { input: InputOf<M> };
        
        type SelectedContextOptions<M> = keyof SelectedContextOf<M> extends never
          ? { context?: never }
          : { context: SelectedContextOf<M> };
        
        type ContextBindingOptions<M> = keyof SelectedContextOf<M> extends never
          ? { contextBindings?: never }
          : { contextBindings: StoryContextBindingsOf<M> };
        
        type StoryContextBindingsOf<M> = {
          [K in keyof SelectedContextOf<M>]:
            ActorRef<ProviderMachineAt<M, K>> | StoryActorRecipe<ProviderMachineAt<M, K>>;
        };
        
        type FixtureOptions<Owner> =
          RequirementsOf<Owner> extends never
            ? { fixtures?: FixtureDefinition[] }
            : {
                fixtures: FixtureTupleClosing<RequirementsOf<Owner>>;
              };
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        type AppStoryOptions<App> = FixtureOptions<App> & {
          maxTurns?: number;
          title?: string;
          description?: string;
          tags?: string[];
        };
        type MachineStoryOptions<M> = InputOptions<M> &
          SelectedContextOptions<M> &
          FixtureOptions<M> & {
            maxTurns?: number;
            title?: string;
            description?: string;
            tags?: string[];
          };
        type ActorRecipeOptions<M> = InputOptions<M> & ContextBindingOptions<M>;
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        type NewCheckpointName<Names extends string, Name extends string> = string extends Name
          ? never
          : Extract<Name, Names> extends never
            ? Name
            : never;
        
        interface StoryPlan<Names extends string = never> {
          checkpoint<const Name extends string>(
            name: NewCheckpointName<Names, Name>,
          ): StoryPlan<Names | Name>;
          run(options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<StoryRun<Names>>;
        }
        
        type StoryRun<Names extends string> = {
          checkpoints: {
            [Name in Names]: CheckpointEvidence;
          };
          end: EndEvidence;
        };
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const common = story
          .machine(editorMachine, options)
          .send(Editor.E.Opened())
          .process()
          .checkpoint("opened");
        
        const saved = common.send(Editor.E.SaveRequested()).process().checkpoint("saved");
        const discarded = common.send(Editor.E.Discarded()).checkpoint("discarded");
        const cancelled = common.send(Editor.E.Cancelled()).checkpoint("cancelled");
        
        const savedRun = await saved.run();
        const discardedRun = await discarded.run();
        savedRun.checkpoints.opened; // valid
        savedRun.checkpoints.saved; // valid
        savedRun.checkpoints.typo; // TypeScript error
        
        // @ts-expect-error duplicate literal checkpoint name
        common.checkpoint("opened");
        
        declare const runtimeName: string;
        // @ts-expect-error widened string cannot erase the known-name map
        common.checkpoint(runtimeName);
        
        // Specification notation for the package-private defensive path only.
        declare function lookupCheckpointInternal<Names extends string>(
          run: StoryRun<Names>,
          name: string,
        ): CheckpointEvidence;
        declare const untrustedCheckpointName: string;
        lookupCheckpointInternal(savedRun, untrustedCheckpointName); // unknown own key throws existing FlowUsageError
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const recipe = story.actor(machine, { input, contextBindings });
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const fixtureDef = fixture({ id: "test-api", implementation: TestApi, seeds });
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const modelDef = model(machineStory, { stateKey: ({ value, memory }) => [value.id, memory] });
        ```

decisions
    D1  One-way literal inference
        Infer definition literals once. Check machine-local structure against that universe.
        Use NoInfer or an equivalent boundary so behavior cannot widen or replace authored states
        or events. Do not require consumer casts or generic wrappers.

    D2  Requirement closure through normalized carriers
        Compute covariant requirements through private acyclic carriers in the
        direction descriptor -> machine binding -> named module -> App. Expose
        RequirementsOf without exposing provider branding or retaining authored
        config as a recursive public carrier.

    D3  Provider implementations remain inert
        Construction composes complete service providers without acquiring them;
        Runtime owns acquisition/finalization. Fixture providers override by
        identity and preserve complete service behavior.

constraints
    C1  Plan ownership and target status
        - @plan.PublicSurface.O2 and @plan.PublicSurface.O3 own public authoring shapes; this Plan
          owns inference and variance. Runtime terminology and identity belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O1
          and @plan.RuntimeV2.O2. Runtime bridge, complete actor/React family, persistence, lifecycle,
          and disposal clauses identified as future targets remain future targets. The temporary
